Bidder Requirements: No specific set-aside certifications or requirements are mentioned in the solicitation details.

Background: The City of Miami is issuing this Invitation to Bid (ITB) for the Caribbean Marketplace Roof Replacement project. This project aims to address structural deficiencies and failures in the existing building envelope at the Little Haiti Cultural Complex. It will not alter the building's size, height, occupancy limit, or intended use. The estimated construction cost is $4.1 million.
Work Details: The scope of work includes:
1) Removal of the existing corrugated metal roofing system down to structural wood trusses due to water intrusion and moisture damage;
2) Repair and reinforcement of existing wood trusses;
3) Removal and replacement of existing roof-mounted HVAC units;
4) Replacement of weathered skylights;
5) Structural reinforcement and repair of damaged concrete joists;
6) Replacement of built-up roofing system, including gutters and downspouts;
7) Replacement of interior lighting fixtures with new LED fixtures;
8) Repair or replacement of exterior roof soffits;
9) Replacement of non-compliant clerestory glazing windows with HVHZ compliant assemblies;
10) New paint and floor finish in select areas. The contractor must self-perform at least 10% of all physical construction labor.
Period of Performance: The contract period will commence upon issuance of the Notice to Proceed and is expected to be completed within a timeframe specified in the contract documents.
Place of Performance: The work will be performed at the Little Haiti Cultural Complex located at 5925 N.E. 2nd Ave, Miami, FL 33137.
Bidder Requirements: Bidders must have a current Certified General Contractor (CGC) license from Florida for a minimum of five years. They must also demonstrate experience as a Prime Contractor on at least three similar projects within the last ten years. Additionally, bidders must self-perform at least ten percent (10%) of the physical labor for the project.

Background: The City of Hollywood, Florida, is seeking proposals from qualified contractors to construct an Emergency Bypass Bar Screen System at the Southern Regional Wastewater Treatment Plant (SRWWTP). This initiative aims to enhance the plant's operational resilience during severe storm events or maintenance periods by allowing for the handling of up to 140 million gallons per day (MGD) of wastewater. 

The project addresses recent failures in the existing bar screen system, which have led to environmental risks and operational challenges.
Work Details: The contractor will be responsible for the following tasks: 
- Installation of a new coarse bar screen structure; 
- Construction of a 72-inch FRP/GRP bypass pipeline; 
- Installation of a new junction box; 
- Extensive utility relocations; 
- Site restoration; 
- Related electrical and control upgrades. 

The work must be executed while maintaining ongoing operations at the plant and adhering to all applicable standards, codes, and permitting requirements. The contractor must demonstrate experience with similar projects, including installation of large diameter pipes under challenging conditions.
Period of Performance: The contract term will commence upon award and is expected to last for 450 days, with potential extensions for two additional one-year terms subject to mutual agreement.
Place of Performance: Southern Regional Wastewater Treatment Plant (SRWWTP), located in Hollywood, Florida.
Bidder Requirements: Bidders must provide evidence of experience in similar work, including installation of at least 100 feet of 48-inch or larger reinforced concrete pipe or equivalent. All subcontractors must be properly licensed and registered with relevant authorities. Bidders must also comply with insurance requirements as specified in the solicitation.

Background: Sarasota County, a political subdivision of the State of Florida, is seeking to select a contractor for the maintenance and clearing of its canal and drainage system assets. The goal of this contract is to ensure the proper upkeep of these essential infrastructure components, which are vital for effective stormwater management and environmental protection.
Work Details: The contractor will provide maintenance and clearing services for Sarasota County's canal and drainage system assets. This includes:

1) Hand clearing of ditches using string type or blade handheld cutting equipment;
2) Canal clearing using mobile mowing equipment or machinery;
3) Cutting, removal, and legal disposal of vegetation including trees measuring four inches in diameter or less;
4) Herbicide treatment of the flow line upon completion of specified maintenance;
5) Monthly maintenance tasks as outlined in Attachment A – Asset List;
6) Removal of all litter from asset banks, slopes, easements, ditches, and fence lines;
7) Compliance with Sarasota County’s Integrated Pest Management (IPM) Guidelines for herbicide applications;
8) Reporting requirements through the County's Computerized Maintenance Management System (CMMS).

The contractor must also ensure that all equipment used is cleaned to prevent the transport of invasive plant species.
Period of Performance: The anticipated initial contract term is three years, with work scheduled monthly according to a proposed work schedule that must be approved by the County.
Place of Performance: The services will be performed within designated site boundaries throughout Sarasota County.
Bidder Requirements: Bidders must provide verifiable references demonstrating experience in similar services within the past seven years. They must comply with all licensing requirements as per Florida State Statute 489.119. Bidders proposing joint ventures must submit documentation attesting to their formation. Additionally, bidders must have at least one employee trained in Florida Green Industries Best Management Practices (BMP). All herbicide applications must comply with Sarasota County’s IPM standards.

Background: The City of Homestead is issuing this Invitation to Bid (ITB) to establish a contract for restroom renovations at the Police Athletic League (PAL) Gym. This facility is crucial for community engagement, providing sports, recreational, and educational programs. The renovations are necessary due to wear and tear over time, focusing on safety, accessibility, and usability.

